    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.is_tree - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.is_tree.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.is_tree()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Check if the graph is a tree.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.is_tree()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Check if the graph is a tree.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.is_tree()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Check if the graph is a tree.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCheck if the graph is a tree.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L25751-L25854){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.is_tree()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Is tree](../assets/graphs/is-tree.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: six nodes connected with five edges and no cycle, so is_tree() returns True. Right: the same graph plus one extra edge between E and F, which closes the cycle A, B, E, F, C (shown in the accent color), so is_tree() returns False.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA tree is a connected, acyclic, undirected graph. For a graph with *n* nodes to be a tree\nit:\n\n- must be undirected\n- must be connected (single component)\n- must have exactly *n - 1* edges\n- must be acyclic\n\nIn practice the method checks the edge count and connectivity, which together guarantee\nacyclicity (see the notes below). A tree has exactly one path between any two nodes,\nwhich is why the property matters in applications such as:\n\n- validating hierarchies (org charts, taxonomies, file systems) stored as undirected\n  graphs\n- confirming that a spanning-tree or network-design result has no redundant links\n- checking a precondition for algorithms that only work on trees\n\nAny self-loop or parallel edge creates a cycle, so a tree is always a simple graph.\nDirected graphs always return `False`, whatever their size; use `is_arborescence()`\nto test for a directed (rooted) tree. Undirected graphs with zero nodes, or one node\nand no self-loop, are treated as trees.\nThe whole graph is checked and the current selection is ignored.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   `True` if graph is a tree, `False` otherwise.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThe four conditions above are not independent: for a connected undirected graph on\n$n$ nodes, any two of \"connected\", \"acyclic\", and \"has exactly $n - 1$ edges\" imply\nthe third, which is why definitions of a tree vary across textbooks yet describe the\nsame object. Directed analogues live under `is_arborescence()`, and if you want to\nallow multiple disjoint components use `is_forest()` instead.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~is_forest`) \n\n:   Whether the graph is a set of disjoint trees.\n\n[](`~is_arborescence`) \n\n:   The directed version of a tree.\n\n[](`~cycle_basis`) \n\n:   Find the independent cycles of an undirected graph.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nA connected, acyclic, undirected graph, like this path, is a tree. Closing it into a cycle\nbreaks the \"acyclic\" rule, and an unconnected node breaks the \"connected\" one:\n\n::: {#92f78edb .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\npath = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\")])\ncycle = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\")])\napart = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\")]).add_node(label=\"C\")\n\npath.is_tree(), cycle.is_tree(), apart.is_tree()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(True, False, False)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nDirection disqualifies a graph, even when its shape is a tree. The same path built as a\ndirected graph returns `False`, while `is_arborescence()` accepts it:\n\n::: {#a4634828 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ndirected_path = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_path_graph(n=3)\n\ndirected_path.is_tree(), directed_path.is_arborescence()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(False, True)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### A tie between peers\n\nA company's reporting lines join each of its 20 people to their manager with 19 edges.\nWithout directions, that's a tree. Ruth and Owen both report to Kwame, so a tie between them\ncloses a triangle, and the graph stops being a tree:\n\n::: {#f1ecd6e0 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\norg = ec.org_chart().to_undirected()\ntied = ec.org_chart().to_undirected().add_edge(\"Ruth\", \"Owen\")\n\norg.is_tree(), tied.is_tree()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(True, False)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe reporting lines with the tie, and the triangle it makes, highlighted:\n\n::: {#a0865442 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ntied.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\",\n    highlight=[\"Kwame\", \"Ruth\", \"Owen\"],\n    highlight_edges=[\n        (\"Ruth\", \"Owen\"), (\"Kwame\", \"Ruth\"), (\"Kwame\", \"Owen\")\n    ],\n    label_by=\"label\",\n    width=820,\n    height=640,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:bloc